M4 Design Note

Each build runs on its own std::thread using a copy of the Workspace and a new 
ProcessingCore. The GUI thread receives the finished corpus and accepts it only 
if its workspace generation still matches the current one.

1. Components and threads

MainWindow (GUI thread): owns the Workspace, the current ProcessingCore, workspace_generation_, and the 
corpus state. Decides whether a finished build is accepted or rejected.

Widgets and SearchResultModel (GUI thread): unchanged from M3.

CorpusBuilder, a new QObject (GUI thread): start(snapshot, generation, delay) launches a job. It owns 
all BuildJobs. When a job reports completion, it joins the thread and emits build_finished(id). Its destructor 
cancels and joins every job.

BuildJob, a new struct (owned on the GUI thread): holds the std::thread, a std::future<ProcessingCore>, 
a std::atomic<bool> cancel flag, and the generation it was started for.

Worker function (worker thread): builds the ProcessingCore and sets the promise (value or exception). 
Its last action is QMetaObject::invokeMethod(builder, "on_job_done", Qt::QueuedConnection, ...). It never touches widgets.

DelayingChunker, a new ChunkingStrategy (worker thread): sleeps delay_ms per document in 10 ms slices, 
checking the cancel flag, then calls the real Chunker. This slows the real build path for --build-delay-ms.

2. Corpus states (GUI thread only)

NotBuilt -> Build Corpus -> Building(g), where g is the current generation.
Building(g) -> success and g is still current -> Current (chunk count shown).
Building(g) -> failure and g is still current -> Failed (error shown).
Current or Failed -> Build Corpus -> Building(g).
Any state -> Add Document or Reset -> NotBuilt, and workspace_generation_ increases.
Any state -> a result whose generation is not current -> ignored, state unchanged.

Search, Inspect, and Context are enabled only in Current. Build Corpus is disabled during Building.

3. Threatening sequences

Build, then Add Document, then the build finishes (R2): generation mismatch, result discarded.
Build, then Reset, then the build finishes (R2): generation mismatch, result discarded.
Build A, Add, Build B, then A finishes before or after B (R2): only B's generation matches.

Duplicate document IDs make rebuild throw (R3): the worker calls set_exception; future.get() rethrows on 
the GUI thread; state becomes Failed.

Search, Inspect, or Context during a build (R2): disabled unless the state is Current.
The Workspace changes while the worker reads it (R2): the worker reads only its own snapshot.
Exit during a build (R4): the CorpusBuilder destructor sets cancel and joins.
Many builds over time (R4): each job is joined and erased when it reports.

Walkthrough A, Add during a build: The Workspace holds {a} and the generation is 1. Build Corpus moves the state to 
Building(1) and starts job J1. The user adds b, so the generation becomes 2, the state becomes NotBuilt, and outputs 
\are cleared. J1 finishes and posts on_job_done. The GUI thread joins J1, and build_finished sees 1 != 2 and discards 
the core. The corpus stays "Not built".

Walkthrough B, Exit during a build: A build is running with a long delay. The user clicks Exit, then Yes, so quit() 
runs and MainWindow is destroyed. The CorpusBuilder destructor sets cancel and calls join(). The worker wakes within 
10 ms, throws BuildCancelled, posts, and returns. join() returns, and the pending queued call is discarded along with 
the QObject. There is no detached thread and no hang.

4. Ownership of one built corpus

1. The GUI thread copies workspace_ into a snapshot and calls start.
2. start keeps the future in a BuildJob and moves the promise and the snapshot into the thread.
3. The worker builds a local ProcessingCore, then promise.set_value(std::move(core)) moves it into the promise's shared state.
4. On the GUI thread, future.get() moves it out, and the BuildJob is erased.
5. If accepted, it is move-assigned into processing_core_, which destroys the old corpus. If stale, the local copy is destroyed.
6. It is destroyed when the next accepted build replaces it, on Reset, or when MainWindow is destroyed.

5. Rejected alternative

Rebuilding processing_core_ in place on the worker and polling an atomic "done" flag. The worker would write the same object 
the GUI reads for Search, which is a data race without a mutex. A stale build would also overwrite the current corpus before 
it could be rejected. Building a separate object and handing it over whole avoids both problems.

